{"task_id": "Mbpp/86", "solution": "```python\nclass ValidationResult:\n    \"\"\"Holds validation outcome and message.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, is_valid: bool, message: str):\n        self.is_valid = is_valid\n        self.message = message\n\nclass CalculationFormula:\n    \"\"\"Metadata for the calculation formula.\"\"\"\n    expression: str = \"3 * n * (n - 1) + 1\"\n\nclass CenteredHexagonalNumber:\n    \"\"\"Stateless class to compute centered hexagonal numbers.\"\"\"\n    def compute(self, n: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Computes the nth centered hexagonal number using the formula.\"\"\"\n        return 3 * n * (n - 1) + 1\n\nclass IntegerParameter:\n    \"\"\"Lightweight wrapper for an integer input parameter.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: int):\n        self.value = value\n\nAssertionExample: str = \"centered_hexagonal_number(10) == 271\"\n\ndef validate_n(n) -> ValidationResult:\n    \"\"\"Validates type and value of n against business rules.\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        return ValidationResult(False, \"Input 'n' must be an integer.\")\n    if n < 1:\n        return ValidationResult(False, \"Input 'n' must be greater than or equal to 1.\")\n    return ValidationResult(True, \"\")\n\ndef centered_hexagonal_number(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the nth centered hexagonal number.\n    Raises ValueError for invalid inputs (non-integer or n < 1).\n    \"\"\"\n    validation = validate_n(n)\n    if not validation.is_valid:\n        raise ValueError(validation.message)\n\n    calculator = CenteredHexagonalNumber()\n    result = calculator.compute(n)\n    return result\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    \"\"\"\n    Demonstrates the public API and verifies the acceptance example.\n    This block executes when the script is run directly.\n    \"\"\"\n    test_n = 10\n    expected_result = 271\n    \n    calculated_result = centered_hexagonal_number(test_n)\n    assert calculated_result == expected_result, \\\n        f\"{AssertionExample} failed: Expected {expected_result}, got {calculated_result}\"\n    \n    print(f\"{AssertionExample} is TRUE. Calculated: {calculated_result}\")\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/87", "solution": "def merge_dictionaries_three(a: dict, b: dict, c: dict) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Merges three dictionaries, validating inputs and raising errors on value conflicts.\n    Returns a new dictionary with the union of keys and agreed values.\"\"\"\n\n    # Validate types of a, b, c as per FR1 and BR1.\n    if not isinstance(a, dict):\n        raise TypeError(\"merge_dictionaries_three: all arguments must be dict; invalid argument for parameter 'a'\")\n    if not isinstance(b, dict):\n        raise TypeError(\"merge_dictionaries_three: all arguments must be dict; invalid argument for parameter 'b'\")\n    if not isinstance(c, dict):\n        raise TypeError(\"merge_dictionaries_three: all arguments must be dict; invalid argument for parameter 'c'\")\n\n    # Compute the union of keys across all three input dictionaries as per FR2 and BR2.\n    union_keys = set(a.keys()) | set(b.keys()) | set(c.keys())\n\n    # Initialize a new dictionary to store the merged result, as per BR5.\n    result = {}\n\n    # Iterate through each unique key to determine its final value.\n    for key in union_keys:\n        values = []\n        # Collect all present values for the current key from the input dictionaries.\n        if key in a:\n            values.append(a[key])\n        if key in b:\n            values.append(b[key])\n        if key in c:\n            values.append(c[key])\n\n        # Check if all collected values are identical as per FR3 and BR3.\n        # If there's only one unique value, all present values are equal.\n        if len(set(values)) == 1:\n            result[key] = values[0]\n        else:\n            # If values differ, raise a ValueError as per FR4 and BR4.\n            # BR7 dictates no implicit precedence, requiring explicit conflict detection.\n            raise ValueError(f\"Conflicting values for key '{key}': {values}\")\n\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/87", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import Any, Dict\n\ndef merge_dictionaries_three(dict1: Dict[Any, Any], dict2: Dict[Any, Any], dict3: Dict[Any, Any]) -> Dict[Any, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Merges three dictionaries using a first-wins precedence rule.\n    Values are taken from dict1, then dict2, then dict3 if a key is present.\"\"\"\n    result = {}\n\n    # Compute the union of keys from all three dictionaries.\n    all_keys = dict1.keys() | dict2.keys() | dict3.keys()\n\n    # Iterate through all unique keys and apply the precedence rule.\n    for key in all_keys:\n        if key in dict1:\n            result[key] = dict1[key]\n        elif key in dict2:\n            result[key] = dict2[key]\n        else:\n            # If key is not in dict1 or dict2, it must be in dict3.\n            result[key] = dict3[key]\n\n    return result\n```"}
