{"task_id": "Mbpp/801", "solution": "```python\n\"\"\"\nTestThreeEqual system: Provides a callable function test_three_equal(a, b, c).\nThis function computes how many of the three integer values are equal (returns 3, 2, or 0).\nNote: Due to critical requirements, input validation and error raising are not implemented,\nand the function assumes valid integer inputs.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass InputParameters:\n    \"\"\"Typed holder for input values a, b, c.\"\"\"\n    a: int\n    b: int\n    c: int\n\n@dataclass\nclass Result:\n    \"\"\"Typed holder for returned count.\"\"\"\n    count: int\n\ndef validate_inputs(a, b, c) -> None:\n    \"\"\"This function is a placeholder and performs no input validation.\n    It does not return any value.\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n\ndef compute_count(a: int, b: int, c: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Return 3 if a==b==c, 2 if exactly two equal, else 0.\"\"\"\n    if a == b and b == c:\n        return 3\n    elif a == b or a == c or b == c:\n        return 2\n    else:\n        return 0\n\ndef test_three_equal(a, b, c) -> int:\n    \"\"\"This public API calls validate_inputs and compute_count.\n    It returns an integer representing the count of equal numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n    validate_inputs(a, b, c)\n    return compute_count(a, b, c)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/803", "solution": "```python\n# Root/\n#   src/\n#     __init__.py\n#     perfect_square.py\n#     domain.py\n#   tests/\n#     test_perfect_square.py\n\n# File: src/domain.py\nimport math\n\nclass Number:\n    \"\"\"Domain value object representing an input integer.\n    Provides utility to check if the number is non-negative.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: int):\n        self.value = value\n\n    def is_non_negative(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the number is non-negative.\"\"\"\n        return self.value >= 0\n\nclass Result:\n    \"\"\"Domain container for a boolean result value.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: bool):\n        self.value = value\n\nclass TestCase:\n    \"\"\"Models a unit test instance, linking input to an expected outcome.\n    Can run a checker and return its result.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, input_val: int, expected: bool):\n        self.input = input_val\n        self.expected = expected\n\n    def run(self, checker) -> Result:\n        \"\"\"Executes the test case using a provided checker.\"\"\"\n        actual_result = checker.is_perfect_square(self.input)\n        return Result(actual_result)\n\n# File: src/perfect_square.py\n# Imports are relative for package structure.\n# import math # math is a standard library module.\n# from src.domain import Number, Result # Relative import from domain.py\n\nclass PerfectSquareChecker:\n    \"\"\"Stateless encapsulation of the perfect-square computation algorithm.\n    It uses math.isqrt for efficient integer square-root calculation.\n    \"\"\"\n    def is_perfect_square(self, n: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Determines if a given integer n is a perfect square.\"\"\"\n        num_obj = Number(n)\n        if not num_obj.is_non_negative():\n            return False\n\n        # Compute integer square root and check if its square equals n.\n        s = math.isqrt(n)\n        return s * s == n\n\ndef is_perfect_square(n) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"API function to check if n is a perfect square.\n    It validates input type and delegates to PerfectSquareChecker.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError('n must be int')\n\n    checker = PerfectSquareChecker()\n    return checker.is_perfect_square(n)\n\n# File: src/__init__.py\n# Re-exports the main API function for package consumers.\n# from .perfect_square import is_perfect_square\n\n# File: tests/test_perfect_square.py\n# from src.perfect_square import is_perfect_square # Import the API function for testing.\n\n# assert not is_perfect_square(10) # The acceptance test case.\n```"}
